## Context

Beek was a Y Combinator-backed startup in Mexico City that brought bestselling English audiobooks to Spanish-speaking listeners in Latin America. Later, creators began uploading their own courses, and the product added listening recommendations. Beek eventually became Musa, which is still live.

## Problem

The core experience was an Audible- or Spotify-style player, and playback had to be reliable, including in the background and from the lock screen. That needed native code.

## Decision

I split React Native features into modules and integrated them through TurboModules into separate iOS and Android host apps, so shared features were written once.

The trade-off: running two native host apps with a small team slowed down platform-specific work. The modular approach paid off most for shared features.

## Architecture

### The native player

I worked mostly on Android (Kotlin), with some iOS (Swift). The module exposed play, pause, seek, background audio, lock-screen controls and offline downloads to React Native.

### Modular React Native

Feature modules sit behind a TurboModule interface, and each platform host app loads them.

### Releases

Fastlane automated Android builds and releases. iOS stayed manual through TestFlight, with hands-on testing before each release.

### The hard moment

Near the end, the product moved to a native wrapper hosting embedded HTML from a Ruby on Rails backend, built as micro-modules. I had little Rails experience and learned it on the job to deliver.

## What I'd do differently

Push earlier for reusing the code we already had, and ramp up on Rails faster.
